  "analysis": "Market read: all 5-day median forecasts are within ±0.25% of zero, confirming the known lack of directional skill — I will not chase medians. What the data does show reliably is risk via interval width: A09 (bonds, 3.24%) and A02 (broad US, 3.76%) have the tightest 80% intervals and the lowest realized vol (10.4% / 8.3%), while the single stocks (A01 13.0%, A07 9.3%, A14 10.2%, A13 9.0%) and gold (A05 9.3%) are the most uncertain. Recent returns show a broad strong 1-day bounce (+1-1.8% in most assets) but mixed 5d/20d, and the portfolio sits in a trivial -0.37% drawdown with no risk-off actions triggered. Strategy: keep the defensive core (bonds ~14.9% + cash ~21.8% ≈ 37% of the portfolio), consolidate the redundant us_broad exposure — A10 is dominated by A02 within the same group (wider interval 5.6% vs 3.8%, higher vol 12.8% vs 8.3%, negative 5d/20d returns vs positive), so I cut A10 to zero and add the proceeds to A02, the highest-quality equity. I keep single-stock exposure only in A15 (narrowest stock interval, lowest stock vol, positive 5d) and leave A01/A13/A07/A14 at zero given their wide intervals and high idiosyncratic vol with no forecast edge. Energy (A03) has strong 20d momentum (+11%) but the highest ETF vol (21.6%) and a wide interval, so it stays small. Net effect: two traded changes (A02 +2.0pts, A10 -2.5pts), cash roughly stable at 21.8%, all group/asset/cash limits satisfied with wide margin.",
